# Showing what you don't know: from tornado diagrams to acceptability curves
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical","b":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical"}
next: {"title":"If we are unsure, should we decide now or pay to learn?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/if-we-are-unsure-should-we-decide-now-or.cast"}

## Scenario theatre: best case, worst case
voice: female
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,6],"y":[0,14]}
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    title: 'Nusinersen ICER: four scenarios (illustrative)'
    row_label: Effect assumption
    col_label: Cost assumption
    row_values:
      - ΔE = 5.0 QALYs
      - ΔE = 1.0 QALY
    col_values:
      - ΔC = 5 mill kr
      - ΔC = 12 mill kr
    cells:
      - [1.0 mill kr/QALY, 2.4 mill kr/QALY]
      - [5.0 mill kr/QALY, 12.0 mill kr/QALY]
    cell_notes:
      - [Best case, null]
      - [null, Worst case]
    highlight:
      - [0, 0]
      - [1, 1]

    axes ax x_label "ΔE — QALYs gained" y_label "ΔC — million kr" hidden true
    curve lam dashed #8a5fa8 expr 1.0*x hidden true
    label lbl_lam "λ = 1 mill kr per QALY" above-left #8a5fa8 attach_to lam hidden true
    curve c_lo dashed #8f887c expr "5" x_from 1 x_to 5 hidden true
    curve c_hi dashed #8f887c expr "12" x_from 1 x_to 5 hidden true
    region box x_from 1 x_to 5 between ["c_lo","c_hi"] style.fill #8f887c style.opacity 0.15 hidden true
    dot p_best #2f6b8f at.x 5 at.y 5 hidden true
    label lbl_best "Best corner: 1.0" below #2f6b8f attach_to p_best hidden true
    dot p_worst #b5482e at.x 1 at.y 12 hidden true
    label lbl_worst "Worst corner: 12.0" right #b5482e attach_to p_worst hidden true
    dot p_a at.x 5 at.y 12 hidden true
    label lbl_a "2.4" left attach_to p_a hidden true
    dot p_b at.x 1 at.y 5 hidden true
    label lbl_b "5.0" right attach_to p_b hidden true

This series is about showing what a health-economic model does not know — and it starts with the worst common way of doing it.
    draw title

Our nusinersen model has two big unknowns: the QALYs the drug adds, and its lifetime cost to the health service.
    draw grid row_title row_header_0 row_header_1 col_title col_header_0 col_header_1

Take both optimistically — five QALYs for five million kroner — and the ICER is one million kroner per QALY.
    draw hl_0_0 cell_0_0

    pause 0.4

Take both pessimistically — one QALY for twelve million — and the same model reports twelve million per QALY.
    draw hl_1_1 cell_1_1 delivery grave

The mixed corners land in between, at two point four and five million per QALY.
    draw cell_0_1 cell_1_0 parallel true

Only that first corner reaches the high-severity threshold we have been working with, a million kroner per QALY.
    highlight cell_0_0 effect glow

These numbers are illustrative — the disagreement was not: Beslutningsforum rejected nusinersen, then accepted it with an age limit.
    point at.ref title gesture underline

    quiz "What does this table tell you about how likely each corner is?"
        intro: A quick check before we move.
        right: Nothing — a scenario table reports four numbers and attaches no probability to any of them.
        wrong: Equal likelihood is something readers assume; the table itself carries no probabilities at all.
        * Each corner is equally likely
        + Nothing at all
        * The base case is most likely

    clear

Same four corners on the plane: effect on the x-axis, cost on the y, and the threshold line through the origin with slope lambda.
    draw ax lam lbl_lam

The best corner lands exactly on that line — even the optimistic story only just buys health at the threshold price.
    draw p_best lbl_best

The worst corner sits far above it, at twelve times the price per QALY.
    draw p_worst lbl_worst

The mixed corners complete a rectangle, and that rectangle is the entire content of the scenario analysis.
    draw p_a lbl_a p_b lbl_b c_lo c_hi box

The base case sits in the middle of it, at about two point eight million kroner per QALY.
    dot p_base #8a5fa8 at.x 3 at.y 8.5
    label lbl_base "Base case: 2.8" right #8a5fa8 attach_to p_base

For the worst corner to be true, both unknowns must hit their pessimistic bound at once — if each is a one-in-twenty event, the pair is one in four hundred.
    highlight p_worst effect glow delivery grave

    quiz "Why is the worst-case corner the least trustworthy number in the table?"
        right: It requires both unknowns at their pessimistic bound simultaneously — far rarer than either bound alone.
        wrong: The threshold and the discount rate are the same in every cell; what changes is that this corner stacks two extremes.
        * It uses the wrong threshold
        + It needs both parameters extreme at the same time
        * It ignores discounting

Drop the corners, and what is left is a range with no density anywhere in it.
    erase p_best lbl_best p_worst lbl_worst p_a lbl_a p_b lbl_b parallel true

The corners are not the plausible extremes — they are the least likely points in the box, given equal billing with the middle.
    label q_label "Where inside the box is the probability?" above attach_to box
    mark q_mark target q_label kind box

Best case and worst case is theatre: the next four parts put the probability back, one parameter at a time in the tornado, all of them together in a thousand draws, and finally as a curve.
    delivery brisk

## Reading the Tornado
voice: female
level: advanced
use: tornado_diagram
with:
    base: -3
    x_label: Net monetary benefit at λ (million kr per patient)
    show_values: true
    title: 'Nusinersen: one-way sensitivity analysis (illustrative)'
    bars:
      - {label: QALY gain vs. care, low: -6.4, high: 0.6}
      - {label: Annual drug price, low: -5.8, high: -0.2}
      - {label: Years on treatment, low: -1.4, high: -4.8}
      - {label: Utility on treatment, low: -4.3, high: -1.8}
      - {label: Discount rate, low: -2.5, high: -3.5}
      - {label: Care costs averted, low: -3.2, high: -2.7}

Best case against worst case is scenario theatre; here is the disciplined version of that instinct — one that tells a committee which assumption the whole decision is hanging on.
    draw title axes

Everything pivots on the base case: at the Norwegian threshold, nusinersen's net monetary benefit sits about three million kroner in the red per patient.
    draw base_line

The magnitudes on this axis are illustrative — the confidential net price is not public — but the ranking and the shape follow the real model's structure.
    highlight base_line effect glow

    pause 0.3

Widest bar first: the QALY gain against current care, swept across its plausible range, moves net benefit from six point four million in the red to just above zero.
    draw bar_0 bar_label_0

Next, the annual drug price — the one lever a negotiation can actually pull — nearly as wide, and never quite reaching black on its own.
    draw bar_1 bar_label_1

Years on treatment swings about three and a half million, because a lifelong intrathecal regimen multiplies price and benefit together.
    draw bar_2 bar_label_2

Utility on treatment — how much better a treated year actually feels — swings two and a half, less than you would expect for a drug sold on quality of life.
    draw bar_3 bar_label_3

And the tail: the discount rate around the guideline four per cent, and the averted costs of ventilation and care, together barely a million and a half.
    draw bar_4 bar_label_4 bar_5 bar_label_5 parallel true

    pause 0.4

Two parameters own almost the whole tornado; the bottom four could all be wrong at once and the sign of the answer would not budge.
    focus bar_0 bar_1 bar_label_0 bar_label_1

So the effect size is where a decision maker's attention, and any research money, belongs.
    mark mark_top target bar_label_0 kind box

    quiz "Which bar tells you most about where extra research money should go?"
        intro: A quick check on how to read this.
        right: The widest bar — here the QALY gain — is the parameter whose resolution could most change the answer.
        wrong: Width is the whole point: the widest bar moves the result furthest, so that uncertainty matters most.
        * The narrowest bar
        + The widest bar
        * The bar nearest zero

Run the same exercise on statins in primary prevention and no single bar crosses zero at all — a tornado that stays on one side of the line is itself the finding.

Now the structural weakness: every bar moves its parameter alone, with all the others frozen at base case.
    highlight bar_0 bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5 effect pulse

Real discounts arrive tied to a duration cap and a stopping rule — move price and years together and the true swing is wider than either bar shows.
    point at.ref bar_1 gesture underline

    pause 0.3

And the ends of a bar are ranges an analyst chose, not outcomes with any likelihood attached.
    point at.ref bar_0 gesture tap

    quiz "What can a tornado diagram NOT tell you?"
        right: It shows how far the result can move, never how probable that move is — the bars carry no probabilities.
        wrong: Ranking and the base case are exactly what it does show; probability is what it withholds.
        * Which parameter swings the result most
        + How likely each swing is
        * The base-case result

A tornado names the assumptions a decision hangs on, and by staying silent about probability, shows exactly why one-way analysis can never be the last word.
    delivery grave

Next: let every parameter move at once, a thousand times over.
    delivery brisk

## The Curve and the Claim
voice: female
level: advanced
domain: {"x":[0,1000],"y":[0,1]}

    axes axes x_label "λ — thousand kr per QALY" y_label P(cost-effective) hidden true
    curve curve_0 #b5482e expr 1/(1+exp(-(x-720)/120)) hidden true
    label curve_label_0 "Nusinersen" right #b5482e attach_to curve_0 hidden true
    curve curve_1 #2f6b8f expr 1-1/(1+exp(-(x-720)/120)) hidden true
    label curve_label_1 "Current care" right #2f6b8f attach_to curve_1 hidden true

A thousand draws scattered across the plane is a cloud no committee can read — so squeeze it into one line that a decision maker can.
    draw axes

The recipe is a sweep: fix a threshold lambda, and count how many of the thousand draws have a positive net monetary benefit — lambda times delta E, minus delta C.
    draw curve_0

That share is one height on the curve for nusinersen; sweep lambda from left to right and the shares join up into this line. Read the axis in thousands of kroner per QALY.
    draw curve_label_0

    pause 0.3

The dashed line marks a half: above it, the drug has the higher net benefit in more runs than it loses.
    path half_line dashed #8f887c points [[0,0.5],[1000,0.5]] data true

At 275,000 kroner per QALY — the ordinary end of the Norwegian range — this illustrative curve sits on the floor: almost no draw makes the drug the better buy.
    path threshold_0 dashed #8f887c points [[275,0],[275,1]] data true
    label threshold_label_0 "275" above #8f887c attach_to threshold_0

    pause 0.3

Push lambda up to 825,000, the top of the severity-adjusted range, and the curve has climbed to roughly seven runs in ten.
    path threshold_1 dashed #8f887c points [[825,0],[825,1]] data true
    label threshold_label_1 "825" above #8f887c attach_to threshold_1

Current care is the exact mirror image: at every lambda one option has the higher net benefit, so the two probabilities always sum to one.
    draw curve_1 curve_label_1

And read the shape: flat across most of the axis, turning inside one narrow band — everywhere outside that band, the answer is not close.
    highlight curve_0 effect glow

The device dates from the mid-1990s, invented to dodge a genuine statistical problem: the ICER is a ratio, and when delta E can sit near zero, its confidence interval stops behaving.
    text title "Nusinersen (illustrative)" x 520 y 715 font_size 26

    pause 0.4

Now the claim itself — probability 0.7 cost-effective — and what it does not say.
    focus curve_0 threshold_1

It is not a clinical probability: it does not say seven in ten children will benefit. The effect size sits inside the model as one uncertain parameter, already averaged over patients.
    delivery grave

Nor is it a claim about whether the drug works: a treatment with a rock-solid, well-measured benefit can still read 0.3, purely because its price outruns lambda.
    highlight threshold_1 effect pulse

What the 0.7 counts is our ignorance about the inputs — utilities, transition probabilities, price, discounting — pushed through the model and tallied at one threshold.

    quiz "The curve reads about 0.7 at lambda = 825,000 kr per QALY. What does that mean?"
        intro: A quick check on the claim.
        right: It is a statement about parameter uncertainty: in seven runs out of ten, lambda times delta E exceeded delta C.
        wrong: The probability lives in the parameters of the model, not at the bedside.
        * 70% of treated children will benefit
        + In 70% of the draws the drug had the higher net benefit
        * The drug is 70% effective

    quiz "The manufacturer halves the price. What happens to nusinersen's curve?"
        right: A lower delta C raises net benefit at every lambda, so the whole curve shifts left.
        wrong: Price enters through delta C, which moves the curve sideways rather than flattening it.
        + It slides left, reaching high probability at a lower lambda
        * It stays put but gets steeper
        * It drops toward zero everywhere

So the acceptability curve answers one question only: at each price we are willing to pay for a year of health, how confident is the model — not whether the drug works.
    highlight curve_0 curve_1 effect glow

## What the Decision Maker Sees
voice: female
level: advanced
use: tornado_diagram
with:
    title: What drives the nusinersen result (illustrative)
    base: -6
    x_label: Net monetary benefit, million kr per patient (illustrative)
    show_values: false
    bars:
      - {label: Duration of effect, low: -14, high: 3}
      - {label: Time horizon, low: -12, high: 1}
      - {label: Drug price, low: -11, high: -1}
      - {label: Discount rate (4%), low: -8, high: -4}
      - {label: Averted care costs, low: -7.5, high: -4.5}

    text t_title "What each display answers" x 500 y 700 font_size 30 hidden true
    text hdr_1 "Display" x 170 y 635 font_size 26 hidden true
    text hdr_2 "The question it answers" #2f6b8f x 475 y 635 font_size 26 hidden true
    text hdr_3 "What it cannot say" #b5482e x 805 y 635 font_size 26 hidden true
    path grid_h #8f887c points [[40,608],[960,608]] hidden true
    path grid_v1 dashed #8f887c points [[320,655],[320,220]] hidden true
    path grid_v2 dashed #8f887c points [[640,655],[640,220]] hidden true
    text n1 "Best / worst case" x 170 y 555 font_size 26 hidden true
    mark x_scenario target n1 kind strike hidden true
    text a1 "Two stories, no middle" #2f6b8f x 475 y 555 font_size 24 hidden true
    text l1 "Nothing about likelihood" #b5482e x 805 y 555 font_size 24 hidden true

Tornado, cloud and curve: line up every display this lecture built and ask what each one honestly answers.
    draw axes base_line title

Back to the nusinersen model in illustrative numbers — net monetary benefit per patient — and the widest bar is how long the treatment effect is assumed to last.
    draw bar_0 bar_label_0

    pause 0.3

Time horizon, price, the four percent discount rate and averted care costs follow, each swing narrower than the one above it.
    draw bar_1 bar_label_1 bar_2 bar_label_2 bar_3 bar_label_3 bar_4 bar_label_4 parallel true

Only that top bar reaches far enough to carry the result across into positive net benefit; the bottom two barely move it at all.
    highlight bar_0 effect glow

    quiz "What does the widest bar of a tornado diagram tell you?"
        intro: A quick check before we synthesise.
        right: The widest bar marks the parameter the result is most sensitive to — nothing about how likely its values are.
        wrong: A tornado bar is a swing between two chosen values, not a likelihood.
        * That parameter's most likely value
        + The result is most sensitive to that parameter
        * The probability the drug is cost-effective

So set the whole toolkit down in one table, and read it column by column.
    clear

    draw t_title hdr_1 hdr_2 hdr_3 grid_h grid_v1 grid_v2

Best case and worst case: two stories with nothing in between, and no claim at all about how likely either one is.
    draw n1 a1 l1

Struck out, because it is the only display here that answers no question a decision maker actually asked.
    draw x_scenario

    pause 0.4

The tornado ranks where to look first — but it moves inputs one at a time, and attaches no probability to any of them.
    text n2 "Tornado" x 170 y 470 font_size 26
    text a2 "Which input matters most" #2f6b8f x 475 y 470 font_size 24
    text l2 "One at a time, no odds" #b5482e x 805 y 470 font_size 24

The cloud of a thousand draws shows how far ΔC and ΔE can land together — but only for the inputs we chose to give a distribution.
    text n3 "The cloud" x 170 y 385 font_size 26
    text a3 "How far ΔC and ΔE land" #2f6b8f x 475 y 385 font_size 24
    text l3 "Only inputs we varied" #b5482e x 805 y 385 font_size 24

And the acceptability curve compresses that cloud into one number per threshold: the share of draws in which the new technology wins.
    text n4 "Acceptability curve" x 170 y 300 font_size 26
    text a4 "P(cost-effective) at λ" #2f6b8f x 475 y 300 font_size 24
    text l4 "Conditional on the model" #b5482e x 805 y 300 font_size 24

    quiz "A CEAC reads 0.7 at the severity-weighted λ. That means:" intro "One more."
        right: It is a property of the simulation: in seven draws out of ten, net benefit at that threshold is positive.
        wrong: It counts draws, not patients — and it says nothing about the model itself.
        * 70% of patients benefit
        + 70% of the simulated draws favour the new drug at that λ
        * We are 70% sure the model is correct

The real limit sits in that last cell: every one of those draws came from the same structure, the same states, the same extrapolation.
    focus l4

All four displays vary the numbers inside the model. Not one of them varies the model.
    text b1 "All four vary the numbers. None vary the model." x 500 y 170 font_size 26

For nusinersen that is the decisive gap — a trial measured in months, projected across a childhood, and no distribution on the parameters can widen that assumption.
    mark b1_box target b1 kind box
    delivery grave

So: scenarios say nothing, the tornado says where to look, the cloud says how wide the answer is, the curve says how often it clears the threshold — and showing what you don't know still leaves what you never modelled.

